<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heinrich Bullinger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1504">1504</a>–<a href="/years/1575">1575</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heinrich Bullinger was a Swiss Reformer who succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a> as head of the Zürich church in 1531 and led it until his death. Through a vast correspondence he advised Reformed churches across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. He agreed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consensus-tigurinus">Consensus Tigurinus</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a> in 1549, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-helvetic-confession">Second Helvetic Confession</a> of 1566 was adopted by Reformed churches from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heinrich-bullinger.jpg" alt="Heinrich Bullinger" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Asper · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heinrich_Bullinger.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>, and Anna Wiederkehr. The couple lived as husband and wife although not legally married, because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Constance waived the penalty for clerical concubinage in return for an annual fee. At eleven he was sent to the St. Martin's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> school in Emmerich, where he studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horace">Horace</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>, and in 1519 he entered the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>. Reading Peter Lombard and the Decretum Gratiani led him to the church fathers, and then to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>. Believing that salvation came through God's grace rather than good works, he was converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> and in 1522 took his Master of Arts degree.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1523 Bullinger became a teacher at the Cistercian monastery of Kappel Abbey, on condition that he take no vows and not attend mass. He began a systematic programme of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> reading, and by 1525 the abbey had abolished mass. He met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a> and Leo Jud in 1523, became Zwingli's friend and ally, and in 1528 was ordained a parish minister of the Reformed church of Zürich.</p><p>On 17 August 1529 he married Anna Adlischweiler, a former nun, and they had eleven children. After Zwingli was killed in the Second Kappel War, Bremgarten was re-catholicised and Bullinger and his family fled, losing almost all their possessions. Three days later he preached in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grossmunster">Grossmünster</a>, and on 9 December 1531 Zürich asked him to be Zwingli's successor as antistes. He kept the office until his death.</p><p>Bullinger opposed war as a means of spreading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. He built the church's government on a synodal order that became a model for other Reformed churches in German-speaking areas, and served as school principal. He wrote against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabaptism">Anabaptists</a> yet allowed them freedom of worship, and no Anabaptist was executed in Zürich during his leadership. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a> he drafted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consensus-tigurinus">Consensus Tigurinus</a> of 1549 on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a>.</p><p>In 1562 he wrote a personal statement of faith that became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-helvetic-confession">Second Helvetic Confession</a> of 1566, adopted by Reformed churches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. He also wrote the Decades, fifty sermons on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-commandments">Ten Commandments</a>, the Creed, the Lord's Prayer and the sacraments.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bullinger died at Zürich on 17 September 1575 and was succeeded as antistes by Zwingli's adopted son Rudolf Gwalther. About 12,000 letters to and from him survive, the most extended correspondence preserved from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, and his writings exceed Luther's and Calvin's combined. He opened Zürich to Protestant fugitives, many of them English, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> between 1550 and 1560 there were 77 editions of the Latin Decades against two of Calvin's Institutes. His idea of the covenant influenced covenant theology.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Heinrich Bullinger” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successor <b>Heinrich Bullinger</b>, head of the Zürich church from 1531 to 1575, corresponded across Europe, agreed the Consensus Tigurinus with John Calvin in 1549 and wrote the Second Helvetic Confession of 1566, adopted by Reformed churches from Hungary to Scotland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In Zurich Zwingli’s work was carried on after his death in battle at Kappel in 1531 by <b>Heinrich Bullinger</b>, and in Strasbourg Martin Bucer worked to reconcile the Lutheran and Swiss reformers before ending his life in exile in Cambridge.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · People</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bremgarten, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. Died in Zurich, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.35,8.34&amp;year=1550" aria-label="Bremgarten on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4745 503 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Heinrich Bullinger"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5195 773h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5195 773h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.35,8.34&amp;year=1550">What was here in 1550? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
